00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.000
To achieve the learning
objectives discussed,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.950
the Building
Conversational Experiences

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.670
With Dialogflow course consists
of these five modules--

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.580
building conversational
experiences with Dialogflow,

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.970
defining intents and
entities, maintaining context

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.160
and taking action,
taking your chatbot

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.810
to production, and
additional functionality.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.920
Conversational experience is the
descriptive comprehensive name

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.900
for a new class
of solutions that

00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:00.800
include chatbots, conversational
apps, and other similar terms.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.390
Dialogflow is a tool
that can help you build

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:05.590
smart conversational agents.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.430
Dialogflow is an end to
end developer platform

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:12.180
for building natural and rich
conversational experiences.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.330
At its core, Dialogflow is
a powerful natural language

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:18.210
understanding engine to
process and understand

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.170
natural language input.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:21.990
In other words,
it lets you easily

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.600
achieve a conversational
user experience

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.900
by handling the natural
language understanding for you.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.410
Using Dialogflow, you can build
conversational experiences

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.620
faster, engage end
users more efficiently,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.870
and maximize reach across
geographies and platforms.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:43.350
By leveraging these capabilities
and what the developer provides

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:45.690
as input training
data, Dialogflow

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.840
creates unique algorithms for
each specific conversational

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.960
agent under the hood, which
continuously learns and is

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:55.020
tweaked for you as more and more
users engage with your agent.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:01.790
Some conversational agents
exist for very specific, very

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:05.480
simple use cases with few
intents and minimal dependency

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.960
on communicating with back end
systems or knowledge bases,

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.450
while some have multiple
intents dealing with more

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.820
complex domains and back ends.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.029
But typically, the workflow to
get your conversational agent

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:20.000
up and running maps out
to these three phases--

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.320
design, what to build, develop,
how to build, and deploy,

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:25.070
how to deliver.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.840
Keep the style
guide in mind as you

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.000
design your agents responses.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.130
For the best user
experience, you probably

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:37.550
don't want your agent to
sound too formal or robotic.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.190
For example, to make your
agent more reminiscent

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:43.220
of actual human conversation,
you might have it say something

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:44.720
like, does that sound good?

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:46.070
Rather than something like--

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:48.290
if you feel you have reached
this message in error.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:55.020
Creating your Dialogflow
agent all starts with intents.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.960
Intents are the trunk
of the dialog tree.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.000
They connect all
of the branches.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.370
Intense determine where
a conversation will go

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.540
and what an agent should do.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:06.510
In communication,
intents can be thought

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:09.240
of as the root verbs
in the conversation,

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.950
such as wanting
coffee translating

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:13.230
to acquiring a beverage.

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:16.320
Sometimes the intents are
not explicit and instead

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.630
are inferred from the entire
composition of a phrase.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:24.250
You want to map your intents to
the goal of your application.

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:26.280
If you are a help
desk application,

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.550
then your intents might include
opening and filing a ticket,

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.950
updating a ticket,
and closing a ticket.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:34.320
But your application
may also need

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.460
to access and update a
user's account information,

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:39.810
branch over to a
live technician,

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:42.990
and even pass along a
quality assurance survey.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:47.310
Even affirmation, answering
yes or no, is an intent.

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.200
Intents evolve as
your understanding

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.060
of the user's needs evolves.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:53.070
And you may find yourself
doubling or tripling

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.560
your intents beyond your
initial set of intents.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.070
To make the task of
defining intents easier,

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:03.350
some rules of thumb
can be applied.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:06.350
First, identify the verbs
in the dialogue people

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:08.240
will have with your agent.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:10.910
Doing that will allow your
agent to have its actions

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:13.740
mapped to needs from the user.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.970
Another possible scenario is to
identify where the application

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:19.010
should branch logic.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:20.690
Here are some examples.

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:22.970
Two mocha coffees, please.

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.130
How many calories in a slice?

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:26.270
I want a pizza.

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:27.890
Or even, I really
need some caffeine.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:32.450
Once you have
chosen your intents,

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:35.310
you need to train your
agent to recognize them.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:38.330
This can be done with the
use of training phrases.

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:40.400
The training phrases
for each intent

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.030
should be representative of
how users will say the intent.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.550
It is always a good
idea to add variations

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:49.280
to the grammatical
construction of a request,

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:51.770
such as passive
versus active voices

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:53.930
or questions versus statements.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:56.240
This will help represent
the variety of ways

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:59.300
a user might say an intent.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.310
When creating an intent,
the more training

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:03.680
phrases you can
think of, the better.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.324
Later, we will
discuss best practices

00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:06.740
for writing your
training phrases.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:12.900
Entities help you get to the
specifics of an interaction.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:16.130
In dialogue, the entities
are the nouns or quantifiers

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:19.220
found through the conversation,
such as a person's name,

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:22.760
the food in a review of a
cruise, specific numbers

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:26.310
or dates just to name a few.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:28.790
When you are creating an
entity and you identify

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:30.590
that the entity
contains attributes

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:34.930
that you need to map to, one way
is to use composite entities.

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:37.370
For our ordering pizza
example, let's say

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:40.310
that we want to create an
intent for ordering pizza.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:44.300
A pizza could be of type
regular or thin crust.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:46.410
Regular and thin
crust, in this case,

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:49.190
both are entries in an
entity called pizza.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:56.670
System entities are pre-built
entities provided by Dialogflow

00:07:56.670 --> 00:08:00.540
in order to facilitate handling
the most popular concepts.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:02.730
Use one of the system
entities to represent

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:05.370
date and time, for
example, or others

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:08.940
such as addresses,
currency, units, numbers,

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:09.780
and many others.

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:16.070
Contexts allow the agent to
keep track of where the user is

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:18.050
at in the conversation.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:20.030
In the context of
Dialogflow, they

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:23.330
are also a means for an
application to store and access

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:26.420
variables mentioned
in the dialog.

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:28.400
In this example
of ordering pizza,

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:30.950
if the sentence, actually,
make that two hours,

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:33.440
is said out of context,
there is no way

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:36.350
that the agent can know that the
user is referring to the pickup

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.240
time for the pizza order.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.090
The context in this conversation
ensures that your Dialogflow

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:43.700
agent knows that this
request is related

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:44.980
to the pizza ordering intent.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:51.530
For the pizza example, we
built our agents step by step,

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:54.370
starting with creating
intents, followed by entities,

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:58.390
and then contexts all done
within the Dialogflow UI.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:00.970
Sometimes you may already
have existing sources that you

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:03.130
can extract entities from--

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:07.310
for instance, a call center
playbook or an FAQ document.

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:09.220
So we look at ways
to leverage this data

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.420
when building our agent.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.200
Sometimes all you need is
a conversation interface

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:16.960
to surface answers from the
existing back end systems

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:18.370
and capabilities.

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:20.710
And so we look at deploying
your back end code

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:23.230
as a webhook on App Engine.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:26.500
We also look at how to
customize your agent's UI,

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:28.480
so that you can have
your own branding.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:32.080
This is where we deploy a
custom front end on App Engine.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:33.960
We also want to
secure our webhook,

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.940
so it only makes authenticated
calls to the back end services.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.400
In our course, we start with
a pizza ordering example

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:45.980
where the back end is meant
to store pizza orders.

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:49.050
So you're mainly writing
into the database.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:51.980
Then we move on to
an HR manual example,

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:54.050
where the back is a
knowledge base where

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:56.750
a query will look
up for definitions

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:58.680
and send it back to the user.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.800
So you're mainly reading
from the database.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:03.590
So how do we build
this knowledge base

00:10:03.590 --> 00:10:05.000
in the first place?

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.070
We use an employee
handbook, which

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:11.420
is an HR manual document with
topic keywords and definitions.

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:13.520
So all we need to
do is leverage that

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:15.470
to build our knowledge base.

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:19.490
In the hands-on lab, we
use Cloud Datalab Notebooks

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:23.330
to quickly run Python scripts to
extract topics from the sample

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:27.230
HR manual, then push them
into a data store entity using

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:30.710
the Cloud Datastore API.

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:33.440
Next, we use Google's
Natural Language API

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:38.360
to form synonyms of these topics
and add them to data store.

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:41.120
Finally, we call
the Dialogflow API

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:44.240
to then populate your
Dialogflow agent's entity list

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:47.312
with these topics from
the data store entity.

00:10:47.312 --> 00:10:48.770
The demo that
follows will walk you

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:51.930
through the steps
covered so far.

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:53.975
So I will pull up
our demo screen.

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:01.570
And I will maximize it.

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:07.400
So I've already done some of
the setup work for the demo

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:09.240
that you would do in the lab.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:12.030
So I've already created
a Datalab instance.

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:14.510
This is actually a
Compute Engine VM that

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:16.820
